/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A capacitor microphone, also called a condenser microphone in some syllabi, has a thin metallised plastic or metal diaphragm.
   - C is capacitance in farads (F); ε₀ = 8.854 × 10⁻¹² F/m is the permittivity of free space; A is the overlapping plate area in square metres (m²); d is the gap in metres (m).
   - ΔC is the change in capacitance in F; C is the initial capacitance in F; x is the diaphragm deflection towards the fixed plate in m; d is the initial gap in m.
   - The back plate has holes or slots so air can move.
   - A simple capacitor microphone needs a steady polarising voltage Vp across the plates.
   - The capacitance change is tiny, so the preamplifier decides performance.
   - Charge amplifier mode: the input is a virtual ground, and the feedback capacitor Cf collects the charge.
   - Voltage preamplifier mode: the input impedance must be very high.
   - Vout is the output voltage magnitude in V; Vp is the polarising voltage in V; ΔC is the capacitance change in F; Cf is the feedback capacitor in F.
   - A capacitive pressure sensor uses a diaphragm as one plate and a fixed plate or deposited electrode as the other.
*/
