/* book exam traps: Connecting a capacitor microphone straight to a low-impedance amplifier. It needs a charge amplifier or a very high input impedance, or the signal collapses. | Ignoring cable capacitance in a voltage-preamplifier design. The cable adds capacitance in parallel, divides the signal, and can make two identical microphones read differently. A charge amplifier at the microphone avoids this. | Mixing electret and externally polarised microphones. An electret has a permanent charge; a plain capacitor microphone needs a polarising supply. | Confusing absolute, gauge and differential pressure ports. A sealed vacuum reference, a vent to atmosphere, and two pressure ports are not interchangeable. | Using the small-change formula for large diaphragm deflection. C = ε₀A/(d − x) becomes nonlinear when x is a large fraction of d. */
